    "html": "<h2>Start from your actual pain</h2>\n<p>Before comparing features, name the pain. Is it the repetitive match (you want month-end to be short)? Is it that you are already on accounting software and just want AR clarity? Or is it collections workflow — reminders and forecasting — not the match itself? The right tool depends on which of these you are solving, and buying a broad platform for a narrow match problem usually means paying for shelves you will not open.</p>\n<h2>Must-have: partials and duplicates as first-class</h2>\n<p>The first filter is blunt: does the tool treat partial payments and duplicates as real states, or as edge cases you code around? A $75-on-$150 invoice should report PARTIAL with the $75 balance, not get forced into paid/unpaid. Duplicates should be flagged, not silently accepted. If a tool cannot show the outstanding balance per client, it is not doing the job.</p>\n<h2>Name-variation tolerance</h2>\n<p>Matching breaks on \"Acme Co\" vs \"Acme Company\". Ask whether the tool normalizes or tolerates name variations, or expects you to pre-clean both lists. <strong>InvoiceReconcile</strong> matches by client name with tolerance for common variations, which removes most false \"unpaid\" flags without a manual cleanup step. If you must hand-align names first, price that time into the decision.</p>\n<h2>Exportable, copy-ready output</h2>\n<p>The reconciliation is only useful if you can use it — paste it into a follow-up, drop it in your books, or hand it to your accountant. Check that the output is copy-ready or exportable rather than trapped in a dashboard you screenshot. InvoiceReconcile drafts a per-client summary you can copy directly, which keeps the result portable.</p>\n<h2>Pricing vs volume</h2>\n<p>Map price to how many reconciliations you actually run. A free tier (InvoiceReconcile offers a free tier with limited daily runs) is enough to validate the fit; a paid tier (InvoiceReconcile Pro is $19/mo) makes sense once volume or the need for an audit log and exports grows. Do not pay platform prices for a single monthly match.</p>\n<h2>Keep a review step</h2>\n<p>Whatever you buy, keep a human sign-off on partials and exceptions. A tool drafts; you approve. The cheapest tool that produces a status you actually review beats an expensive one whose output you trust blindly. Reconciliation is decision-support, not a certified result.</p>\n",
