    "description": "Alternatives to manual reconciliation range from full accounting suites to lightweight matchers. This guide maps the categories — accounting software, spreadsheets, and specialized tools like InvoiceReconcile — and where each one fits.",
    "html": "<h2>What \"alternatives\" actually covers</h2>\n<p>When people search for invoice reconciliation alternatives, they usually mean one of four things: a full accounting suite (Xero, QuickBooks), a generic spreadsheet, a broader AR/collections platform, or a focused matcher that does one job well. They are not interchangeable — they differ in weight, price, and how much bookkeeping baggage they drag along.</p>\n<h2>Full accounting software</h2>\n<p>Xero and QuickBooks include reconciliation as part of a general ledger, bank feeds, and reporting. If you already run your books there, the matching lives where your data already is, which is convenient. The trade-off is that you adopt (or already run) a full accounting system to get the reconciliation feature, and the matching is built for bank-to-ledger more than invoice-to-payment nuance.</p>\n<h2>Spreadsheets</h2>\n<p>A spreadsheet is the default alternative and the most flexible. You control every column and rule. The cost is that you build and re-verify the logic each period, and a single error compounds across the whole close. For low volume it can be enough; for growing AR it becomes the bottleneck.</p>\n<h2>Broader AR / collections platforms</h2>\n<p>Some platforms widen the lens to dunning, reminders, and cash-flow forecasting on top of matching. They can help if collections workflow is your pain, not just the match. The downside is scope and price — you pay for capabilities you may not need to answer \"who owes me what this month.\"</p>\n<h2>Focused matchers like InvoiceReconcile</h2>\n<p>A lightweight matcher takes your invoice list and your payment (or Stripe) export and returns PAID / PARTIAL / UNPAID with balances, duplicate and balance checks, and a copy-ready summary. <strong>InvoiceReconcile</strong> is built for exactly this: paste two lists, get a per-client status. It is not a ledger and not a collections suite — it owns the matching step so month-end stays short. For founders who close the books themselves, that narrow focus is usually the fit.</p>\n<h2>How to choose</h2>\n<p>Pick by where the pain is. Already on Xero/QuickBooks and happy? Use the built-in match. Need only the monthly status and want it fast? A focused matcher is lighter. Need dunning and forecasting too? Weigh a broader platform. Whatever you pick, keep a human review step — the tool drafts, you sign off.</p>\n",
